# Single-Port RAM with a Self-Checking Testbench (Verilog HDL)

## About this project

I designed an 8 x 16-bit single-port RAM in Verilog and wrote a testbench that checks it on its own and prints PASS or FAIL, so I don't have to read waveforms to know whether it works.

The part I found most interesting is that reads and writes share a single data bus. That means the RAM has to drive the bus only when it is being read and let go of it the rest of the time, so the testbench can use the same wires to write.

## Features

- 8 locations, 16 bits each
- Write on the rising clock edge, read straight away (no clock needed)
- One shared (inout) data bus that floats (high-Z) when the RAM is not driving it
- Asynchronous reset that clears all 8 locations
- Self-checking testbench with a reference copy of the memory
- Deliberate bug test: I broke the write logic on purpose to prove the testbench catches it

## How the RAM works

| wr_en | rd_en | What happens |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | 0 | Write: `data` is stored at address `add` on the rising clock edge |
| 0 | 1 | Read: the stored word appears on `data` right away |
| 0 | 0 | Nothing happens, and `data` floats (high-Z) |
| 1 | 1 | Nothing happens, and `data` floats (high-Z) |

## Ports

| Port | Direction | Width | Purpose |
|---|---|---|---|
| `clk` | input | 1 | Clock for writes |
| `rst` | input | 1 | Asynchronous reset |
| `wr_en` | input | 1 | Write enable |
| `rd_en` | input | 1 | Read enable |
| `add` | input | 3 | Address (8 locations) |
| `data` | inout | 16 | Shared data bus |

Vivado's elaborated (RTL) schematic reports 150 cells, 23 I/O ports and 188 nets. It shows eight 16-bit registers with asynchronous clear, a read multiplexer, and a tri-state buffer on the data bus.

## How I tested it

The testbench keeps its own copy of what the memory should hold (`mem_cpy`) and compares every read against it. It uses three tasks, `reset`, `write` and `read`, and a `drive` signal so the testbench can put data on the shared bus only while writing.

Test sequence:

1. Reset, then read all 8 locations (all should be 0)
2. Write all 8 locations with random data
3. Read all 8 back
4. 20 random writes
5. Read all 8 again
6. Reset, then read all 8 (all 0 again)

For every read it prints `ok: address N gave XXXX`, or a failure line showing the value it expected. At the end it prints `PASS` or `FAIL: n errors`.

| Setting | Value |
|---|---|
| Clock period (`cycle`) | 20 ns |
| Hold time (`thold`) | 5 ns |
| Setup time (`tsetup`) | 5 ns |
| Memory | 8 locations x 16 bits |

## Results

The run ends with `PASS` at 1870 ns, and the `error` counter stays at 0 the whole time.

![Console, PASS at 1870 ns](screenshots/RAM_PASS.png)

![Full run, error stays 0](screenshots/RAM_res.png)

**The deliberately broken design: 16 errors**

A testbench is only trustworthy once you have seen it fail. In `ram.v` I changed `wr_en&&!rd_en` to `!wr_en&&!rd_en`, so writes never happen. The testbench reported 16 errors: 8 on the reads after the first writes and 8 on the reads after the random writes.

![Console, failures reported](screenshots/RAM_fail.png)

![Broken run, error reaches 16](screenshots/RAM_resu.png)

