## What I learned

- How a shared inout bus works: one side drives it while the other leaves it at high-Z.
- Why asynchronous reset needs `posedge rst` in the sensitivity list.
- An early version of my testbench printed PASS even for a broken RAM, because it never counted errors. Breaking the design on purpose is how I found that out, and now I do it for every testbench.

## What I would add next

- A check that the bus really floats when the RAM is idle
- A test for what happens when both enables are high
- A reset in the middle of an operation
- Depth and width as parameters
- Assertions, once I learn SystemVerilog

## Tools used

- Verilog HDL
- Xilinx Vivado (behavioral simulation and RTL schematic)

## How to run

In Vivado, add `ram.v` as a design source and `self_tb.v` as a simulation source, then choose Run Simulation, Run Behavioral Simulation. Vivado stops at 1000 ns by default, so type `run all` in the Tcl Console to reach the end of the test.

